#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

# ==============================================================================
# deck - AI-assisted slide production
# ==============================================================================
#
# TWO LAYERS, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS.
#
#   THE GATE    _deck/src: the renderer and the geometry linter. Authoritative
#               text and layout live in HTML/CSS on a fixed 16:9 pixel canvas,
#               rendered by the installed Google Chrome to PDF and rasterized
#               to PNG. Because the layout is real DOM, every geometry check is
#               a measurement rather than an opinion.
#
#   THE PRODUCT this file: a launcher that starts ONE Claude Code agent which
#               interviews the PO in Japanese, reads the source material,
#               agrees the agenda and the exact copy with them, WRITES
#               storyboard.yaml itself, and then drives the gates above -
#               fixing what the linter finds and re-running it.
#
# The gate shipped first, which left the PO hand-writing storyboard.yaml. That
# is rubocop without a programmer. The deterministic commands now live behind
# `deck gate <name>` - they are the agent's gates, not the PO's interface - and
# the PO's interface is a sentence:
#
#     deck "野沢温泉 次回MTG資料"
#
# ONE AGENT, NOT THREE. squad runs two independent review lenses because
# judging code is subjective and a second reader sees what the first missed.
# Here the review IS `deck lint`: nine rules over measured geometry, the same
# answer every time. A second opinion pane would spend tokens to re-read a
# deterministic result.
#
# Like squad and vet, the PO runs this through a ~/.local/bin symlink, so
# _deck has to be found relative to the resolved real path and not to $0.

usage() {
  local status="${1:-1}"
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage: deck <title> [--source <notion-url|file>] [options]
       deck check [run-id|part of a title]
       deck list
       deck resume [run-id]
       deck close [run-id]
       deck rm <run-id>...
       deck clean
       deck gate <command> [args...]
       deck gen-helpers <dir>

Start an AI agent that interviews you in Japanese, reads your source material,
agrees the agenda and the exact wording with you, writes the storyboard itself
and produces the deck. You never open storyboard.yaml.

Example:
  deck "野沢温泉 次回MTG資料"
  deck "営業会議 11月" --source https://www.notion.so/...
  deck "定例ふりかえり" --source ./notes.md --when 来週火曜

Launch options:
  --source <ref>   A Notion URL, or a path to a file. May be repeated. When
                   omitted the agent asks for it, and will also take text
                   typed straight into the pane.
  --purpose <text> What the deck is for. Asked if not given.
  --audience <who> Who is in the room. Asked if not given.
  --when <text>    Which meeting, and when. Free text: 来週火曜 is fine.
  --dir <path>     Where to create the deck workspace. Default:
                   $DECK_DECKS_ROOT/<date>-<title>, or the deck you are
                   already standing in.
  --theme <name>   Theme for a new workspace. Default: watercolor.

Reviewing the result:
  deck check            Open the newest rendered deck's PDF, or pick one when
                        there are several. The PDF is the artefact to read; the
                        PNGs are for the linter and for pasting single slides.
  deck check <what>     A run id, or any part of a title.

Lifecycle, as squad has:
  deck list             List retained runs, their phase and their status.
  deck resume           Resume the most recent retained run.
  deck resume <run-id>  Resume a specific run. State, approvals and position
                        survive a close: the agent is told which gate it had
                        reached.
  deck close            Close the run for the current tmux window.
  deck rm <run-id>...   Delete closed runs' artifacts. Never the deck itself.
  deck clean            Delete ALL run artifacts that are not running.

The gates (deterministic, no agent, no network):
  deck gate init <dir>      Scaffold a deck workspace.
  deck gate doctor          Chrome, poppler, fonts, node. Installs nothing.
  deck gate source          Was the material fully retrieved? A partial fetch
                            BLOCKS, because an agenda built on half the
                            minutes looks exactly like one built on all of it.
  deck gate ledger          Does every extracted quote really occur in the
                            source? Checked, not trusted.
  deck gate agenda          Is every still-open item either on the agenda or
                            explicitly excluded with a reason?
  deck gate copy            The readable Japanese copy sheet for the content
                            gate, each line marked quoted or written.
  deck gate fidelity        Do the storyboard's numbers, dates and table cells
                            trace back to the source?
  deck gate validate        Schema and semantics of the storyboard.
  deck gate approve agenda|content   Record the approved revision's digest.
  deck gate render [--release]       storyboard -> HTML -> PDF -> PNGs.
  deck gate lint            The layout gate, on measured geometry.
  deck gate prompts --single         ONE pasteable illustration brief.
  deck gate inbox           Import whatever was dropped in <deck>/inbox.
  deck gate export          Verify and list the deliverable.
  deck gate status          Phase, approvals, slots.

  `deck gate` with no arguments prints the gate layer's own help. The old
  spellings (`deck render`) are gone: a bare verb is now a deck title, and
  guessing between the two would eventually guess wrong. Use `deck -- <title>`
  if your deck is really called "render".

Inspection:
  deck gen-helpers <dir>
                   Write the generated helpers and the agent prompt into <dir>
                   and exit. Launches nothing, so it is the way to inspect or
                   test what a run would generate.

Environment:
  DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS  Hard cap on lint -> fix -> lint rounds. A positive
                          integer; default 3. At the cap the agent reports to
                          you instead of starting another round.
  DECK_DECKS_ROOT         Where new deck workspaces are created.
                          Default: $HOME/decks.
  DECK_STATE_ROOT         Cross-run state and run artifacts.
                          Default: ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/deck.
  DECK_HOME               Where deck's Node sources, schemas and themes live.
  DECK_NODE               The node binary to use. Default: node from PATH.
  DECK_AGENT              The agent binary. Default: claude.
EOF
  exit "$status"
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ resolution

script_real_path() {
  local src="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" dir
  while [[ -L "$src" ]]; do
    dir="$(cd -P "$(dirname "$src")" && pwd)"
    src="$(readlink "$src")"
    [[ "$src" == /* ]] || src="$dir/$src"
  done
  printf '%s/%s\n' "$(cd -P "$(dirname "$src")" && pwd)" "$(basename "$src")"
}
DECK_SCRIPT_PATH="$(script_real_path)"
DECK_SCRIPT_DIR="$(dirname "$DECK_SCRIPT_PATH")"
DECK_HOME="${DECK_HOME:-$DECK_SCRIPT_DIR/_deck}"
export DECK_HOME

NODE_BIN="${DECK_NODE:-node}"
DECK_AGENT="${DECK_AGENT:-claude}"

# The state root is the Node side's too, so the two agree on one location
# without the launcher having to tell it.
deck_state_root() {
  if [[ -n "${DECK_STATE_ROOT:-}" ]]; then
    printf '%s' "$DECK_STATE_ROOT"
  else
    printf '%s/deck' "${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}"
  fi
}
DECK_RUN_ROOT="$(deck_state_root)/runs"
DECK_DECKS_ROOT="${DECK_DECKS_ROOT:-$HOME/decks}"

require_node_sources() {
  if [[ ! -f "$DECK_HOME/src/cli.js" ]]; then
    echo "Error: deck's Node sources are missing." >&2
    echo "       Looked for: $DECK_HOME/src/cli.js" >&2
    echo "       deck resolved its own path to: $DECK_SCRIPT_PATH" >&2
    echo "       Set DECK_HOME if _deck lives somewhere else." >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  if ! command -v "$NODE_BIN" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "Error: node was not found on PATH (looked for '$NODE_BIN')." >&2
    echo "       deck needs Node 20.11 or newer. Set DECK_NODE to point at it." >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  # Dependencies are committed as a lockfile, not as node_modules. Say so
  # plainly rather than letting Node fail with a bare MODULE_NOT_FOUND.
  if [[ ! -d "$DECK_HOME/node_modules/playwright-core" ]]; then
    echo "Error: deck's dependencies are not installed." >&2
    echo "       Run: npm install --prefix $DECK_HOME" >&2
    exit 2
  fi
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- run state

# The newest run, by the START TIME IT RECORDED.
#
# This used to be `sort | tail -1` over the FULL PATH, and a path begins with
# the deck directory name - so a run under "zebra-deck" from last week
# outranked one under "ab-deck" from a minute ago, and `deck resume` with no
# argument picked the wrong deck. The recorded STARTED_AT is what "latest"
# means.
latest_run_state_file() {
  find "$DECK_RUN_ROOT" -mindepth 3 -maxdepth 3 -name state.env -type f 2>/dev/null |
    while IFS= read -r f; do
      unset STARTED_AT
      # shellcheck disable=SC1090
      source "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
      printf '%s\t%s\n' "${STARTED_AT:-0000-00-00 00:00:00}" "$f"
    done |
    sort |
    tail -n 1 |
    cut -f2-
}

load_run() {
  local requested_id="${1:-}"
  local allow_latest="${2:-true}"
  local state_file=""

  if [[ -n "$requested_id" ]]; then
    # MATCHED BY PATH, not by a loop.
    #
    # This used to pipe every state.env through a `while read` whose body was
    # `[[ test ]] && printf`. On a non-matching run that body exits 1, so the
    # loop exited 1, so the command substitution exited 1 - and `set -e` then
    # killed deck inside the assignment, with no message at all. `deck resume
    # <run-id>` therefore died silently for every run that was not the first
    # one `find` happened to return.
    state_file="$(
      find "$DECK_RUN_ROOT" -mindepth 3 -maxdepth 3 -path "*/$requested_id/state.env" -type f 2>/dev/null |
        head -n 1
    )" || state_file=""
    # AN EXPLICIT ID THAT DOES NOT EXIST IS AN ERROR, not an invitation to
    # pick something else. Falling back meant a mistyped id silently resumed,
    # closed or REMOVED a different deck's run.
    [[ -n "$state_file" && -f "$state_file" ]] || {
      printf 'Error: no deck run with id "%s".\n' "$requested_id" >&2
      printf '       deck will not fall back to another run for an id you typed.\n' >&2
      printf '       See: deck list\n' >&2
      exit 1
    }
  elif [[ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]]; then
    state_file="$(tmux show-option -wqv @deck_state_file 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi

  if [[ ( -z "$state_file" || ! -f "$state_file" ) && "$allow_latest" == "true" ]]; then
    state_file="$(latest_run_state_file)"
  fi

  [[ -n "$state_file" && -f "$state_file" ]] || {
    echo "Error: no matching deck run found (see: deck list)" >&2
    exit 1
  }

  # state.env is generated by deck itself and contains shell-escaped values.
  # shellcheck disable=SC1090
  source "$state_file"
  STATE_FILE="$state_file"
}

write_state() {
  mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR"
  {
    printf 'RUN_ID=%q\n' "$RUN_ID"
    printf 'STARTED_AT=%q\n' "$STARTED_AT"
    printf 'TASK=%q\n' "$TASK"
    printf 'DECK_DIR=%q\n' "$DECK_DIR"
    printf 'RUN_DIR=%q\n' "$RUN_DIR"
    printf 'DECK_BIN=%q\n' "$DECK_SCRIPT_PATH"
    printf 'AGENT_SESSION_ID=%q\n' "$AGENT_SESSION_ID"
    printf 'REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS=%q\n' "${REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS:-$DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS_DEFAULT}"
    printf 'WINDOW_SHORT_NAME=%q\n' "${WINDOW_SHORT_NAME:-deck}"
    printf 'WINDOW_ID=%q\n' "${WINDOW_ID:-}"
    printf 'CURRENT_WINDOW_NAME=%q\n' "${CURRENT_WINDOW_NAME:-}"
    # NON-AUTHORITATIVE, exactly as squad records its panes: tmux assigns a new
    # pane id on every resume. Kept for diagnostics only; nothing addresses a
    # pane through it.
    printf 'AGENT_PANE=%q\n' "${AGENT_PANE:-}"
    # THE RUN IS LIVE IF THIS PROCESS IS. deck does not own a tmux window - it
    # runs in whatever window the PO was already in - so window existence is
    # not liveness: a resume records the PO's long-lived shell window, and that
    # window outlives the run. The launcher process is the run.
    printf 'LAUNCHER_PID=%q\n' "${LAUNCHER_PID:-}"
    printf 'PURPOSE=%q\n' "${PURPOSE:-}"
    printf 'AUDIENCE=%q\n' "${AUDIENCE:-}"
    printf 'MEETING_WHEN=%q\n' "${MEETING_WHEN:-}"
    printf 'SOURCE_SPECS=%q\n' "${SOURCE_SPECS:-}"
    # WHERE THE PO WAS STANDING when they launched. A --source of ./notes.md
    # means "relative to here", and the launcher then cds into the deck
    # workspace before starting the agent - so without recording this, a
    # relative source path pointed at a file that was never there.
    printf 'INVOKED_FROM=%q\n' "${INVOKED_FROM:-}"
    printf 'THEME=%q\n' "${THEME:-watercolor}"
    printf 'RUN_STATUS=%q\n' "${RUN_STATUS:-running}"
  } > "$RUN_DIR/state.env.tmp.$$"
  # Replaced by rename, so a concurrent reader - `deck list` in another
  # window, a close while the launcher is live - never sees a half-written
  # state file.
  mv -f "$RUN_DIR/state.env.tmp.$$" "$RUN_DIR/state.env"
  STATE_FILE="$RUN_DIR/state.env"
}

DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS_DEFAULT=3

# The hard cap on lint -> fix -> lint rounds. Without it the loop has no bound:
# a finding the agent cannot actually fix buys another full round, forever, and
# the PO never hears about it. Same validation idiom as squad's, deliberately:
# validated before anything is created, message to stderr, exit 1.
# THE RECORDED CAP WINS ON A RESUME.
#
# This used to take the environment or the default unconditionally, and resume
# called it after load_run - so a run launched with DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS=7
# came back with a cap of 3, and the agent was told a limit the PO had never
# set. The recorded value is part of the run.
#
# An explicitly set DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS still wins, because the PO changing
# it is a deliberate act; it is reported when it differs from the record.
#
# @param $1  "resume" to prefer the recorded value
resolve_repair_max_rounds() {
  local mode="${1:-launch}" recorded="${REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS:-}"
  if [[ "$mode" == "resume" && -n "$recorded" && -z "${DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS:-}" ]]; then
    REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS="$recorded"
  else
    REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS="${DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS:-${recorded:-$DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS_DEFAULT}}"
  fi
  [[ "$REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
    echo "Error: DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS must be a positive integer." >&2
    exit 1
  }
  if [[ "$mode" == "resume" && -n "$recorded" && "$recorded" != "$REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS" ]]; then
    printf 'Note: this run recorded a repair cap of %s; DECK_REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS is set, so %s is used.\n' \
      "$recorded" "$REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS"
  fi
}

# The tmux WINDOW name to use before the agent has decided what this run is
# about. DISPLAY ONLY: runs are resolved by WINDOW_ID and by the @deck_run_id
# pane option, never by this string.
placeholder_window_name() {
  printf 'deck:%s' "${RUN_ID:0:15}"
}

# Is the run still running? The launcher process is the run: it starts the
# agent in the foreground and outlives nothing.
run_is_live() {
  local pid="${1:-}"
  [[ -n "$pid" ]] || return 1
  kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null
}

window_exists() {
  local target_window_id="${1:-}"
  [[ -n "$target_window_id" ]] || return 1
  command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  tmux list-windows -a -F '#{window_id}' 2>/dev/null |
    awk -v target="$target_window_id" '$0 == target { found = 1 } END { exit !found }'
}

# Where the run has got to, read from the DECK rather than from the run record.
#
# The phase is the Node side's answer, re-derived from what is on disk, so a
# run whose agent died still reports the truth: the approvals and the rendered
# output are the facts, and a run record that disagreed with them would be the
# thing that misleads the PO.
run_phase() {
  local deck_dir="${1:-}"
  [[ -n "$deck_dir" && -f "$deck_dir/deck.yaml" ]] || { printf 'no-deck'; return 0; }
  local out
  out="$(cd "$deck_dir" && "$NODE_BIN" "$DECK_HOME/src/cli.js" status --json 2>/dev/null)" || {
    printf 'unknown'
    return 0
  }
  printf '%s' "$out" | "$NODE_BIN" -e '
let s = ""; process.stdin.on("data", (d) => { s += d; });
process.stdin.on("end", () => {
  try { process.stdout.write(String(JSON.parse(s).phase || "unknown")); }
  catch { process.stdout.write("unknown"); }
});' 2>/dev/null || printf 'unknown'
}

# Open a deck's finished PDF.
#
# The artefact the PO actually reviews is out/deck.pdf - the PNGs exist for the
# linter's measurements and for pasting single slides, not for reading the deck.
# Without this, checking a deck meant remembering the workspace name, typing
# `open ~/decks/<name>/out/deck.pdf`, and getting it wrong when the name
# contained Japanese.
#
# With no argument: the newest deck that has a PDF, or a picker when fzf is
# installed and there is more than one. A run id or a unique part of a title
# also works.
check_run() {
  local wanted="${1:-}"
  local state_file chosen_pdf chosen_label
  local -a labels=() pdfs=()

  while IFS= read -r state_file; do
    unset RUN_ID RUN_DIR DECK_DIR TASK
    # shellcheck disable=SC1090
    source "$state_file"
    [[ -n "${DECK_DIR:-}" && -f "$DECK_DIR/out/deck.pdf" ]] || continue
    local title
    title="$(cat "$RUN_DIR/title.txt" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "${TASK:-unknown}")"
    # A run id or any part of the title narrows the list; nothing matches
    # everything.
    if [[ -n "$wanted" ]]; then
      [[ "$RUN_ID" == *"$wanted"* || "$title" == *"$wanted"* ]] || continue
    fi
    labels+=("$RUN_ID  $title")
    pdfs+=("$DECK_DIR/out/deck.pdf")
  done < <(
    find "$DECK_RUN_ROOT" -mindepth 3 -maxdepth 3 -name state.env -type f 2>/dev/null | sort -r
  )

  if [[ ${#pdfs[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
    if [[ -n "$wanted" ]]; then
      echo "deck: no rendered deck matches '$wanted'." >&2
    else
      echo "deck: no rendered deck yet." >&2
    fi
    echo "deck: 'deck list' shows every run and the phase it reached;" >&2
    echo "deck: a deck has a PDF once it reaches 'rendered'." >&2
    return 1
  fi

  if [[ ${#pdfs[@]} -eq 1 ]]; then
    chosen_pdf="${pdfs[0]}"
    chosen_label="${labels[0]}"
  elif command -v fzf >/dev/null 2>&1 && [[ -t 0 ]]; then
    local picked
    picked="$(printf '%s\n' "${labels[@]}" | fzf --reverse --no-sort --prompt 'deck > ')" || return 0
    [[ -n "$picked" ]] || return 0
    local i
    for i in "${!labels[@]}"; do
      if [[ "${labels[$i]}" == "$picked" ]]; then
        chosen_pdf="${pdfs[$i]}"
        chosen_label="${labels[$i]}"
        break
      fi
    done
  else
    # No fzf, or not a terminal: the newest wins, and says which it took so the
    # PO can name another.
    chosen_pdf="${pdfs[0]}"
    chosen_label="${labels[0]}"
    echo "deck: ${#pdfs[@]} rendered decks; opening the newest." >&2
  fi

  printf '%s\n' "$chosen_label"
  printf '%s\n' "$chosen_pdf"
  open "$chosen_pdf"
}

list_runs() {
  local state_file title status phase waiting
  printf '%-22s  %-9s  %-16s  %-4s  %s\n' "RUN ID" "STATUS" "PHASE" "WAIT" "TITLE"
  find "$DECK_RUN_ROOT" -mindepth 3 -maxdepth 3 -name state.env -type f 2>/dev/null |
    sort -r |
    while IFS= read -r state_file; do
      unset RUN_ID RUN_STATUS RUN_DIR WINDOW_ID DECK_DIR TASK LAUNCHER_PID
      # shellcheck disable=SC1090
      source "$state_file"
      title="$(cat "$RUN_DIR/title.txt" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "${TASK:-unknown}")"
      status="${RUN_STATUS:-unknown}"
      if run_is_live "${LAUNCHER_PID:-}"; then
        status="running"
      elif [[ "$status" == "running" ]]; then
        status="closed"
      fi
      phase="$(run_phase "${DECK_DIR:-}")"
      waiting="-"
      [[ -e "$RUN_DIR/awaiting-po.env" ]] && waiting="[?]"
      printf '%-22s  %-9s  %-16s  %-4s  %s\n' "$RUN_ID" "$status" "$phase" "$waiting" "$title"
    done
}

# True when a tmux on PATH is one of deck's own kill-guard shims rather than
# the real binary. The marker is the wrapper's own message, written further
# down, so the two stay in sync.
is_tmux_guard_shim() {
  [[ -n "$1" && -f "$1" ]] && grep -q 'deck: blocked dangerous tmux command' "$1" 2>/dev/null
}

# The REAL tmux binary, skipping deck's kill-guard shims.
#
# The agent runs with a PATH shim that refuses kill-server/kill-session/
# kill-window/kill-pane, and this resolves what that shim should exec. It has
# to skip shims rather than take the first tmux on PATH, because deck can be
# launched from inside an already-guarded pane and `command -v tmux` would then
# find the outer shim - a shim execing a shim refuses everything.
#
# Unlike squad, deck has no carve-out to make: nothing in deck kills a window.
deck_real_tmux() {
  local candidate
  if [[ -n "${DECK_REAL_TMUX:-}" ]] && ! is_tmux_guard_shim "$DECK_REAL_TMUX"; then
    printf '%s' "$DECK_REAL_TMUX"
    return 0
  fi
  # Every candidate is checked, because a nested guard can capture a shim in
  # DECK_REAL_TMUX and `command -v` would then find the outer shim first.
  while IFS= read -r candidate; do
    if ! is_tmux_guard_shim "$candidate"; then
      printf '%s' "$candidate"
      return 0
    fi
  done < <(type -aP tmux 2>/dev/null || true)
  return 1
}

# THE INVARIANT: whenever the awaiting-PO marker's presence CHANGES, the tmux
# window name is recomposed from the current base. No exceptions.
#
# tmux only repaints a window name when something calls rename-window, so
# unlinking the marker on its own leaves the [?] badge on screen with nothing
# left that will ever repaint it - the file says "not waiting" while the PO
# still sees "waiting", and they would believe the badge. Every clearing path
# therefore goes through await-po, which is the single place that does both
# halves.
clear_awaiting_po() {
  local run_dir="$1"
  if [[ -x "$run_dir/await-po" ]]; then
    "$run_dir/await-po" clear 2>/dev/null || true
  else
    # A run created before await-po existed has no helper to call. It also has
    # no marker and no badge, so removing the file directly is right there.
    rm -f "$run_dir/awaiting-po.env"
  fi
}

# CLOSE IS NOT DESTRUCTIVE HERE, and that is a deliberate difference from
# squad.
#
# squad's close kills the run's tmux window, which is right there: squad
# CREATED that window and split three panes into it, so the window is the
# squad. deck creates no window. It runs one agent in the pane the PO was
# already standing in, which is usually their own shell - and on a resumed run
# that pane is recorded in WINDOW_ID. Killing it would close a window the PO
# never gave deck, taking whatever else was in it.
#
# So close records the lifecycle state, takes the waiting badge down, and says
# what is still running. Stopping a live agent is the PO's call, in the pane
# they can see.
close_run() {
  load_run "${1:-}" true
  clear_awaiting_po "$RUN_DIR"
  local was_live=false
  run_is_live "${LAUNCHER_PID:-}" && was_live=true
  # THE LIVE PROCESS IDENTITY SURVIVES A CLOSE.
  #
  # This used to clear LAUNCHER_PID unconditionally while deliberately leaving
  # the agent running - so the next `resume` saw a dead run, launched a SECOND
  # agent against the same run directory, and `rm`/`clean` would happily
  # delete helpers the live agent was still executing. Close marks the
  # lifecycle; only the launcher itself, on its way out, clears its own pid.
  RUN_STATUS="closed"
  write_state
  printf 'Closed deck run %s.\n' "$RUN_ID"
  printf 'The deck itself is untouched at: %s\n' "$DECK_DIR"
  if [[ "$was_live" == "true" ]]; then
    printf '\nNOTE: the agent for this run is STILL RUNNING in its own pane (pid %s).\n' "$LAUNCHER_PID"
    printf '      deck does not kill windows or panes - it did not create them.\n'
    printf '      Stop it there with Ctrl-C if you want it to stop now.\n'
    printf '      Until it exits, resume and rm are refused: a second agent on the\n'
    printf '      same run would have two writers in one deck.\n'
  fi
  printf '\nResume with: deck resume %s\n' "$RUN_ID"
}

remove_run() {
  local run_id="$1"
  load_run "$run_id" false
  if run_is_live "${LAUNCHER_PID:-}"; then
    echo "Error: run $RUN_ID is still running (pid $LAUNCHER_PID); close it first." >&2
    return 1
  fi
  # THE DECK IS NEVER DELETED. It is the PO's document: they asked to remove a
  # RUN, which is the agent's scratch space, the prompt and the gate records.
  rm -rf "${RUN_DIR:?}"
  printf 'Removed run artifacts for %s.\n' "$RUN_ID"
  printf 'The deck is untouched at: %s\n' "$DECK_DIR"
}

clean_runs() {
  local state_file ids=() reply total=0
  while IFS= read -r state_file; do
    unset RUN_ID WINDOW_ID LAUNCHER_PID
    # shellcheck disable=SC1090
    source "$state_file"
    total=$((total + 1))
    run_is_live "${LAUNCHER_PID:-}" || ids+=("$RUN_ID")
  done < <(find "$DECK_RUN_ROOT" -mindepth 3 -maxdepth 3 -name state.env -type f 2>/dev/null | sort)

  if [[ ${#ids[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
    # Two different situations, and saying the wrong one is a small lie the PO
    # would have to go and check.
    if [[ "$total" -eq 0 ]]; then
      echo "Nothing to clean: there are no retained runs."
    else
      echo "Nothing to clean: all $total retained run(s) are still running."
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  printf 'This will delete the run artifacts of %d run(s):\n' "${#ids[@]}"
  printf '  %s\n' "${ids[@]}"
  printf 'The decks themselves are NOT deleted.\n'
  printf 'Continue? [y/N] '
  read -r reply
  [[ "$reply" == "y" || "$reply" == "Y" ]] || { echo "Cancelled."; return 0; }
  local id
  for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
    remove_run "$id" || true
  done
}

tag_pane() {
  local pane="$1" role="$2"
  command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  [[ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]] || return 0
  tmux set-option -p -t "$pane" @deck_run_id "$RUN_ID" 2>/dev/null || true
  tmux set-option -p -t "$pane" @deck_role "$role" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# gen-helpers writes a fixed set of filenames with plain redirection, and
# redirection FOLLOWS a symlink already sitting at the target name. A reused or
# hostile directory could therefore have deck overwrite any file the caller can
# write, just by pre-placing helpers/await-po as a symlink to it.
#
# The cheap fix, chosen over resolving every output path: the target must be a
# directory this command can OWN - absent, or present and empty. One check
# covers every output name at once, including names added later, and an empty
# directory cannot contain a symlink to follow.
require_own_gen_dir() {
  local dir="$1"
  if [[ -e "$dir" && ! -d "$dir" ]]; then
    echo "Error: $dir exists and is not a directory." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if [[ -d "$dir" ]] && [[ -n "$(ls -A "$dir" 2>/dev/null)" ]]; then
    echo "Error: $dir is not empty." >&2
    echo "       deck gen-helpers writes a fixed set of file names, and will not" >&2
    echo "       overwrite anything already sitting at one of them. Use a new" >&2
    echo "       directory." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
}

# --------------------------------------------------------- generated helpers
# Regenerated on every launch AND on every resume, so a run created by an older
# version of this script gets the current helpers as soon as it is resumed.
# None of them may contain a tmux pane ID.

generate_helper_scripts() {
  mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR"

  # --- Display width ----------------------------------------------------------
  # squad uses jq for this because jq is one of its hard dependencies and the
  # awk on macOS is not Unicode-aware. deck's hard dependency is node, so the
  # same codepoint table lives in a tiny node script instead: `cut -c` counts
  # CHARACTERS, and would let a ten-character Japanese name occupy twenty
  # columns.
  cat > "$RUN_DIR/window-width.mjs" <<'HELPER'
// Display columns, not characters. One Japanese character costs two columns.
const RANGES = [
  [0x1100, 0x115F], [0x2E80, 0x303E], [0x3041, 0x33FF], [0x3400, 0x4DBF],
  [0x4E00, 0x9FFF], [0xA000, 0xA4CF], [0xAC00, 0xD7A3], [0xF900, 0xFAFF],
  [0xFE30, 0xFE6F], [0xFF00, 0xFF60], [0xFFE0, 0xFFE6],
  [0x1F300, 0x1F9FF], [0x20000, 0x3FFFD],
];
const cw = (cp) => (RANGES.some(([a, b]) => cp >= a && cp <= b) ? 2 : 1);
const cols = (s) => [...s].reduce((n, ch) => n + cw(ch.codePointAt(0)), 0);

const [mode, text = '', maxArg = '20'] = process.argv.slice(2);
const max = Number(maxArg);
if (mode === 'width') {
  process.stdout.write(`${cols(text)}\n`);
} else if (mode === 'fit') {
  if (cols(text) <= max) {
    process.stdout.write(`${text}\n`);
  } else {
    // Two columns are reserved for the ellipsis, so the result still fits.
    let w = 0; let out = '';
    for (const ch of text) {
      const c = cw(ch.codePointAt(0));
      if (w + c > max - 2) break;
      w += c; out += ch;
    }
    process.stdout.write(`${out}..\n`);
  }
} else {
  process.stderr.write('Usage: window-width.mjs <width|fit> <text> [max]\n');
  process.exit(2);
}
HELPER

  # --- Window name ------------------------------------------------------------
  {
    printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n'
    printf 'set -euo pipefail\n'
    printf 'RUN_DIR=%q\n' "$RUN_DIR"
    printf 'NODE_BIN=%q\n' "$NODE_BIN"
    printf 'WINDOW_FALLBACK_NAME=%q\n' "$(placeholder_window_name)"
    cat <<'HELPER'
# Usage:
#   window-name display [base]   the name to hand to tmux rename-window
#   window-name fit <text> [max] width-aware truncation
#   window-name width <text>     display columns of a string

# How wide the tmux window name may be, in DISPLAY COLUMNS, not characters. A
# window name sits in a list beside other windows, so this is a hard budget:
# one Japanese character costs two columns, so 20 columns is about ten of them.
SHORT_NAME_MAX_COLUMNS=20

# Shown while the agent is waiting on the PO. Deliberately ASCII: it costs
# exactly four columns in every terminal, where an emoji's width depends on the
# font and would make the budget below a guess. To change it, change this one
# line - the marker is never stored in the base name.
AWAITING_PO_MARKER='[?] '

measure() {
  "$NODE_BIN" "$RUN_DIR/window-width.mjs" "$1" "$2" "${3:-0}"
}

case "${1:-}" in
  fit)
    [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { printf 'Usage: window-name fit <text> [max-columns]\n' >&2; exit 2; }
    measure fit "$2" "${3:-$SHORT_NAME_MAX_COLUMNS}"
    ;;
  width)
    [[ $# -eq 2 ]] || { printf 'Usage: window-name width <text>\n' >&2; exit 2; }
    measure width "$2"
    ;;
  display)
    BASE="${2:-}"
    # short-name.txt is the stored base, written by update-title on every title
    # change and seeded at launch. The baked-in fallback covers a run whose
    # first title update has not happened yet.
    if [[ -z "$BASE" && -s "$RUN_DIR/short-name.txt" ]]; then
      BASE="$(head -n 1 "$RUN_DIR/short-name.txt")"
    fi
    [[ -n "$BASE" ]] || BASE="$WINDOW_FALLBACK_NAME"
    # PRESENCE of the marker file is the truth; its contents are detail only,
    # so a half-written or hand-edited file still renders correctly.
    if [[ -e "$RUN_DIR/awaiting-po.env" ]]; then
      MARKER_COLUMNS="$(measure width "$AWAITING_PO_MARKER")"
      # The marker spends part of the SAME budget, so the composed name still
      # fits the window list. The base is re-fitted rather than pre-truncated,
      # which is why clearing the marker restores the full stored base.
      printf '%s%s\n' "$AWAITING_PO_MARKER" \
        "$(measure fit "$BASE" "$((SHORT_NAME_MAX_COLUMNS - MARKER_COLUMNS))")"
    else
      measure fit "$BASE" "$SHORT_NAME_MAX_COLUMNS"
    fi
    ;;
  *)
    printf 'Usage: window-name <display|fit|width> [args]\n' >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac
HELPER
  } > "$RUN_DIR/window-name"
  chmod +x "$RUN_DIR/window-name"

  # --- Awaiting-PO marker -----------------------------------------------------
  # Records that the agent has asked the PO something and is waiting.
  #
  # WHY A FILE OF ITS OWN. Not RUN_STATUS: that feeds `deck list` and the
  # closed/running lifecycle, and "running but waiting" is not a lifecycle
  # state. Not a state.env key: write_state rewrites a fixed set of variables
  # wholesale, so a key written by a helper while deck itself is mid-write
  # would be lost. An independent file, replaced by rename, has neither problem
  # and is readable by anything.
  #
  # TMUX IS OPTIONAL HERE, unlike in squad. deck splits no panes, so it can run
  # in a plain terminal; the marker file is the durable truth and the window
  # rename is best effort. A missing tmux costs the badge, not the record.
  {
    printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n'
    printf 'set -euo pipefail\n'
    printf 'RUN_DIR=%q\n' "$RUN_DIR"
    cat <<'HELPER'
# Usage:
#   await-po mark [reason...]   about to ask the PO something; raise the marker
#   await-po clear              the PO has been heard from; lower it
#   await-po status             print the current state (debugging)
#
# Both mark and clear are IDEMPOTENT. The agent runs `clear` at the start of
# every turn without knowing whether a marker exists, so clearing nothing must
# be silent and free.

MARKER="$RUN_DIR/awaiting-po.env"

# Re-apply the window name for the CURRENT marker state. Best effort on
# purpose: the marker file is the durable truth and is already written by the
# time this runs, so a tmux that cannot be reached must not turn into a failed
# command in the agent's face.
apply_window_name() {
  local display
  command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  [[ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]] || return 0
  display="$("$RUN_DIR/window-name" display 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  tmux rename-window "$display" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# One safe line: no control characters (so the file stays parseable), no
# runaway length, whitespace collapsed. The value is additionally written with
# %q below, so quotes, $, backticks and the like are inert even when sourced.
sanitize_reason() {
  local cleaned
  cleaned="$(printf '%s' "$1" |
    tr '\n\r\t' '   ' |
    sed -E 's/[[:cntrl:]]//g; s/^[[:space:]]+//; s/[[:space:]]+$//; s/[[:space:]]+/ /g')"
  # NOT `cut -c`: that counts BYTES unless the locale says otherwise, and a
  # Japanese reason split mid-character comes back out of %q as octal escapes.
  # window-name truncates by display column, in node, which is Unicode-aware
  # wherever it runs.
  "$RUN_DIR/window-name" fit "$cleaned" 120 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "$cleaned"
}

case "${1:-}" in
  mark)
    shift
    REASON="$(sanitize_reason "${*:-}")"
    GATE="${DECK_GATE_LABEL:-}"
    # Written to a temp file and RENAMED, so a reader never observes a
    # half-written marker. $$ keeps two concurrent marks from sharing a temp
    # path; rename is atomic within the directory, so the last writer wins.
    TMP="$MARKER.tmp.$$"
    {
      printf 'AWAITING_PO=1\n'
      printf 'AWAITING_PO_AT=%q\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
      [[ -n "$REASON" ]] && printf 'AWAITING_PO_REASON=%q\n' "$REASON"
      [[ -n "$GATE" ]] && printf 'AWAITING_PO_GATE=%q\n' "$GATE"
    } > "$TMP"
    mv -f "$TMP" "$MARKER"
    apply_window_name
    if [[ -n "$REASON" ]]; then
      printf 'Awaiting PO: %s\n' "$REASON"
    else
      printf 'Awaiting PO.\n'
    fi
    ;;
  clear)
    rm -f "$MARKER"
    apply_window_name
    ;;
  status)
    if [[ -e "$MARKER" ]]; then
      printf 'awaiting-po: YES\n'
      cat "$MARKER"
    else
      printf 'awaiting-po: no\n'
    fi
    printf 'window name would be: %s\n' \
      "$("$RUN_DIR/window-name" display 2>/dev/null || printf '(unavailable)')"
    ;;
  *)
    printf 'Usage: await-po <mark [reason...]|clear|status>\n' >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac
HELPER
  } > "$RUN_DIR/await-po"
  chmod +x "$RUN_DIR/await-po"

  # --- Title update -----------------------------------------------------------
  {
    printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n'
    printf 'set -euo pipefail\n'
    printf 'RUN_DIR=%q\n' "$RUN_DIR"
    cat <<'HELPER'
# Usage: update-title <new-title-file> <new-short-name-file>
#
# Two files, one command, because a long pane title and a short window name go
# stale independently and the window list is how the PO tells their windows
# apart.
[[ $# -eq 2 ]] || { printf 'Usage: update-title <title-file> <short-name-file>\n' >&2; exit 2; }
[[ -s "$1" ]] || { printf 'update-title: %s is empty\n' "$1" >&2; exit 2; }
[[ -s "$2" ]] || { printf 'update-title: %s is empty\n' "$2" >&2; exit 2; }

head -n 1 "$1" > "$RUN_DIR/title.txt"
head -n 1 "$2" > "$RUN_DIR/short-name.txt"

if command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 && [[ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]]; then
  # Composed through window-name so the awaiting-PO marker survives a title
  # change: the marker is never stored in the base name, and this is the only
  # place that puts the two together.
  tmux rename-window "$("$RUN_DIR/window-name" display)" 2>/dev/null || true
  tmux select-pane -T "$(head -n 1 "$RUN_DIR/title.txt")" 2>/dev/null || true
fi
printf 'title:      %s\n' "$(head -n 1 "$RUN_DIR/title.txt")"
printf 'short name: %s\n' "$(head -n 1 "$RUN_DIR/short-name.txt")"
HELPER
  } > "$RUN_DIR/update-title"
  chmod +x "$RUN_DIR/update-title"

  # --- One gate ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # squad's helpers write a <step>-status.txt holding the PROCESS status next to
  # a <step>-result.txt holding the output, because a helper can produce
  # pass-shaped output and still have died. deck's Node commands already do
  # that inside the workspace; this records the same split in the RUN
  # directory, so the run has its own audit trail of which gate ran when and
  # what it said - including for a run whose deck is later edited by hand.
  {
    printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n'
    printf 'set -uo pipefail\n'
    printf 'RUN_DIR=%q\n' "$RUN_DIR"
    printf 'DECK_DIR=%q\n' "$DECK_DIR"
    printf 'DECK_BIN=%q\n' "$DECK_SCRIPT_PATH"
    cat <<'HELPER'
# Usage: deck-gate <gate> [args...]
#
# Runs one deterministic gate in the deck workspace and records its result.
# Returns the gate's own exit status, so the agent reads 0/1/2/3/4 exactly as
# `deck gate` defines them:
#   0 completed and passed   1 completed and FAILED its verdict
#   2 usage   3 environment  4 the gate itself failed and has no verdict
[[ $# -ge 1 ]] || { printf 'Usage: deck-gate <gate> [args...]\n' >&2; exit 2; }
GATE="$1"
# A gate name is a file name component here, so it may not wander out of the
# run directory via a crafted argument.
case "$GATE" in
  */*|..|.|'') printf 'deck-gate: %s is not a gate name\n' "$GATE" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
LABEL="$GATE"
# `approve content` and `approve agenda` are two different gates with two
# different records, so the record is named after both words.
case "$GATE" in
  approve|assets|prompts) [[ -n "${2:-}" ]] && LABEL="$GATE-${2#--}" ;;
esac
RESULT="$RUN_DIR/gate-$LABEL-result.txt"
STATUS="$RUN_DIR/gate-$LABEL-status.txt"

# Cleared BEFORE the run, so a reader can never pick up the previous round's
# status for this round's gate. Same reason squad's send-to clears the status
# file before queueing the keystrokes.
rm -f "$STATUS"

{
  printf '=== deck gate %s ===\n' "$*"
  printf 'at:   %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
  printf 'deck: %s\n\n' "$DECK_DIR"
} > "$RESULT"

cd "$DECK_DIR" || { printf '4\n' > "$STATUS"; printf 'deck-gate: cannot enter %s\n' "$DECK_DIR" >&2; exit 4; }
"$DECK_BIN" gate "$@" >> "$RESULT" 2>&1
CODE=$?
printf '%s\n' "$CODE" > "$STATUS"
cat "$RESULT"
exit "$CODE"
HELPER
  } > "$RUN_DIR/deck-gate"
  chmod +x "$RUN_DIR/deck-gate"

  # --- The self-repair cycle --------------------------------------------------
  # THE CAP IS MECHANICAL, NOT A PROMISE.
  #
  # squad caps review rounds by having a helper - classify-diff - decide which
  # round it is and tell the Manager, rather than asking the Manager to count.
  # Same reasoning here: a cap the agent polices by remembering how many times
  # it has looped is a cap that an agent with a long transcript will miscount,
  # and the failure mode is an unbounded token burn the PO is not watching.
  #
  # The round number lives in a file. This helper increments it, refuses to run
  # past the cap, and prints a machine-readable block the agent reads instead
  # of deciding.
  {
    printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\n'
    printf 'set -uo pipefail\n'
    printf 'RUN_DIR=%q\n' "$RUN_DIR"
    printf 'DECK_DIR=%q\n' "$DECK_DIR"
    printf 'NODE_BIN=%q\n' "$NODE_BIN"
    printf 'DECK_HOME=%q\n' "$DECK_HOME"
    printf 'CAP=%q\n' "$REPAIR_MAX_ROUNDS"
    cat <<'HELPER'
# Usage:
#   build-cycle            run one round: render, then lint, then report
#   build-cycle --status   print where the loop is without running anything
#   build-cycle --reset    start the count again (after re-approved wording)
#
# Exit status:
#   0  the round ran and lint PASSED
#   1  the round ran and lint FAILED - fix the findings and run another round
#   3  REFUSED: the cap is spent. Report to the PO; do not loop again.
#   4  the round could not complete (render died, no deck)
ROUND_FILE="$RUN_DIR/repair-round.txt"

read_round() {
  if [[ -s "$ROUND_FILE" ]]; then head -n 1 "$ROUND_FILE"; else printf '0'; fi
}

# A RESET IS BOUND TO A NEWLY APPROVED REVISION.
#
# --reset used to erase the counter unconditionally, which made the cap
# advisory rather than mechanical: an agent out of rounds could reset and keep
# going, and nothing recorded that it had. A reset is legitimate when the PO
# has approved a NEW revision of the wording - that is fresh work, and it
# earns a fresh allowance. The digest approved at the last reset is
# remembered, and a second reset against the same digest is refused.
approved_content_digest() {
  cd "$DECK_DIR" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  "$RUN_DIR/deck-gate" status --json 2>/dev/null | "$NODE_BIN" -e '
let s = ""; process.stdin.on("data", (d) => { s += d; });
process.stdin.on("end", () => {
  try {
    const j = JSON.parse(s.slice(s.indexOf("{")));
    const c = (j.approvals || {}).content || {};
    process.stdout.write(c.approved && c.matches ? String(c.approved_digest) : "");
  } catch { process.stdout.write(""); }
});' 2>/dev/null
}

case "${1:-}" in
  --reset)
    RESET_MARK="$RUN_DIR/repair-reset-digest.txt"
    DIGEST="$(approved_content_digest)"
    if [[ -z "$DIGEST" ]]; then
      printf 'round=%s\ncap=%s\nreset=refused\n' "$(read_round)" "$CAP"
      printf '\nRefusing to reset the repair counter: this deck has no CURRENT content\n' >&2
      printf 'approval. A reset buys another %s round(s), and it is earned by the PO\n' "$CAP" >&2
      printf 'approving a new revision of the wording - not by asking for one.\n' >&2
      printf 'Show the copy sheet, get approval, run approve content, then reset.\n' >&2
      exit 3
    fi
    if [[ -s "$RESET_MARK" ]] && [[ "$(head -n 1 "$RESET_MARK")" == "$DIGEST" ]]; then
      printf 'round=%s\ncap=%s\nreset=refused\n' "$(read_round)" "$CAP"
      printf '\nRefusing to reset again for the SAME approved revision.\n' >&2
      printf 'The counter was already reset for content digest %s.\n' "${DIGEST:0:12}" >&2
      printf 'If the wording needs to change again, change it, show the PO, and get\n' >&2
      printf 'approval for the new revision.\n' >&2
      exit 3
    fi
    printf '0\n' > "$ROUND_FILE"
    printf '%s\n' "$DIGEST" > "$RESET_MARK"
    printf 'round=0\ncap=%s\nreset=yes\napproved_digest=%s\n' "$CAP" "$DIGEST"
    exit 0
    ;;
  --status)
    ROUND="$(read_round)"
    printf 'round=%s\ncap=%s\nfinal_round=%s\n' "$ROUND" "$CAP" \
      "$([[ "$ROUND" -ge "$CAP" ]] && printf 'yes' || printf 'no')"
    exit 0
    ;;
esac

ROUND="$(read_round)"
NEXT=$((ROUND + 1))
if [[ "$NEXT" -gt "$CAP" ]]; then
  printf 'round=%s\ncap=%s\nfinal_round=yes\nrefused=yes\n' "$ROUND" "$CAP"
  printf '\nThe repair cap of %s round(s) is spent. Do NOT run another round.\n' "$CAP" >&2
  printf 'Mark the run as awaiting the PO and report the outstanding findings.\n' >&2
  exit 3
fi
printf '%s\n' "$NEXT" > "$ROUND_FILE"

# A PREVIEW render, never --release. A release render is refused when the
# content digest has moved, and during repair it often has: that refusal
# belongs at delivery, not in the middle of the loop.
"$RUN_DIR/deck-gate" render > "$RUN_DIR/cycle-$NEXT-render.txt" 2>&1
RENDER=$?
if [[ "$RENDER" -ne 0 ]]; then
  printf 'round=%s\ncap=%s\nfinal_round=%s\nrender_status=%s\nlint_verdict=none\n' \
    "$NEXT" "$CAP" "$([[ "$NEXT" -ge "$CAP" ]] && printf 'yes' || printf 'no')" "$RENDER"
  printf '\nThe render did not complete; there is no geometry to lint.\n' >&2
  printf 'See %s\n' "$RUN_DIR/cycle-$NEXT-render.txt" >&2
  exit 4
fi

"$RUN_DIR/deck-gate" lint > "$RUN_DIR/cycle-$NEXT-lint.txt" 2>&1
LINT=$?

# Everything below is read out of the lint report rather than scraped from the
# human output, so a wording change in a message cannot change what the agent
# is told. Node does the JSON, because node is already a hard dependency.
SUMMARY="$("$NODE_BIN" --input-type=module -e '
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
// WHAT COUNTS AS BLOCKING IS DECIDED BY THE LINTER, NOT BY THIS HELPER.
// A first version re-derived it here as "not info and not warn", which
// counted 8 findings of severity suggestion as blocking and printed
// blocking=8 beside lint_verdict=pass. Two copies of a severity rule is one
// copy too many, so the predicate is imported from the linter itself and the
// totals come from the report the linter already wrote.
// NOTE: no apostrophes below. This whole block is a single-quoted shell
// string, and one apostrophe closes it and feeds the rest to bash.
const [deckDir, deckHome] = process.argv.slice(1);
const { isBlocking } = await import(`${deckHome}/src/lint/finding.js`);
const out = (k, v) => process.stdout.write(`${k}=${v}\n`);
let report = null;
try {
  report = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(deckDir, "out", "lint", "report.json"), "utf8"));
} catch {}
if (!report) { out("lint_verdict", "unreadable"); out("blocking", "?"); process.exit(0); }
const findings = report.findings || [];
const blocking = findings.filter(isBlocking);
// REPAIRABLE NOW, vs OUTSTANDING ILLUSTRATIONS.
//
// An empty slot blocks the lint verdict, because a deck with no pictures in
// it is not finished and the whole point of the slot-fill rule is to say so.
// But the illustrations are step 7 and this loop is step 6, so an agent that
// treated them as repair work would spend its entire cap on findings it
// cannot act on yet and then refuse to go on. The two counts are therefore
// reported separately, and the exit code below is about blocking_layout -
// the work that can be done in THIS round.
//
// This is not a second opinion about severity. The linter decides what
// blocks, and lint_verdict still says fail; this only splits the blocking
// set by which step of the flow can clear it.
const slotFindings = blocking.filter((f) => f.rule === "slot-fill");
const layout = blocking.filter((f) => f.rule !== "slot-fill");
out("lint_verdict", report.verdict || "unknown");
out("blocking", (report.counts && report.counts.blocking !== undefined) ? report.counts.blocking : blocking.length);
out("blocking_layout", layout.length);
out("blocking_slots", slotFindings.length);
out("suggestions", (report.counts && report.counts.suggestions) || 0);
out("rules", [...new Set(layout.map((f) => f.rule))].sort().join(",") || "-");
out("slides", [...new Set(layout.map((f) => f.slide).filter(Boolean))].sort().join(",") || "-");
' "$DECK_DIR" "$DECK_HOME" 2>/dev/null)"

# The content approval state comes from `deck gate status --json`, which
# re-derives it from what is on disk rather than reading back a stored phase.
DIGEST_BLOCK="$(cd "$DECK_DIR" && "$RUN_DIR/deck-gate" status --json 2>/dev/null | "$NODE_BIN" -e '
let s = ""; process.stdin.on("data", (d) => { s += d; });
process.stdin.on("end", () => {
  try {
    const j = JSON.parse(s.slice(s.indexOf("{")));
    const c = (j.approvals || {}).content || {};
    process.stdout.write(`phase=${j.phase}\n`);
    process.stdout.write(`content_approved=${c.approved ? "yes" : "no"}\n`);
    process.stdout.write(`content_digest_matches=${c.matches ? "yes" : "no"}\n`);
